//! Caravan abate -- a plan takes down the wall its reader released it from,
//! rather than hearing a person say they will carry the matter and holding the
//! boundary against them anyway.
//!
//! `answer.rye` opened the last door: the word a reader hands back about the
//! wall they were called to leaves their own box and lands where an operator
//! reads the outcome, so a correspondence that reached a person finally hears
//! what that person said.
//!
//! **And then the wall stands, exactly as it stood before they spoke.** A
//! reader who answers `takes_up` has said the matter leaves with them, and the
//! plan's own boundary answers to nobody: it stands in the notes run after run,
//! outliving the correspondence it ended and the person who released it. A word
//! that changes nothing is a word nobody needed to write.
//!
//! **The measurement is walls a plan kept standing after their reader took the
//! matter up.** The rung below ends a reader's reply going unread; this one ends
//! the reply, once read, meaning nothing.
//!
//! **Abating is taking down, and taking down is a record.** A run that abates
//! writes eleven bytes into the plan's own notes -- the recorded answer echoed
//! whole and this run's own byte last -- reads that note back before it believes
//! a word of it, and only then lets the wall fall.
//!
//! **A run abates only a wall whose answer it actually opened.** A plan that
//! heard nobody holds no word to act on, and a record found standing from a run
//! before today answers some other matter entirely. `AbateUnopened` refuses by
//! name.
//!
//! **A wall comes down only on the word of the reader who was called to it.** A
//! reader who lets the boundary stand has asked for nothing, and taking it down
//! on their acceptance would read a release into a word that granted none.
//! `AbateUnreleased` refuses by name.
//!
//! **The abatement names the wall it takes down.** A plan may still be standing
//! a boundary about some other matter, and reading this reader's release as
//! covering it would take down a wall nobody released. `AbateMismatched` is the
//! rung itself.
//!
//! **The record lands before the wall falls.** The abatement is written first
//! and the boundary comes down after, so a run falling between the two leaves a
//! wall standing beside a record of why it should not -- exactly the state the
//! rung below always left -- rather than a boundary gone with nothing saying who
//! released it.
//!
//! **Abating is not settling, and never answering for anybody.** The run acts on
//! one word and rewrites nothing else: the settlement, the quarrel, its age, its
//! standing, the case on the desk, the finding, the plan's own word, the
//! concession, the meeting, the redress, the delivery, the reader's first
//! verdict, the second look, the telling, the reader's reply, the mark, the
//! call, and the reader's own answer each come out of an abating run exactly as
//! they went in -- and both runs settle `carried out whole`. The reader's word
//! stays in the reader's own hand; the plan obeys it and may never write it.
//!
//! Five refusals of this rung's own, matching the five below it.
//! `AbateUnopened` is the moment a wall may come down at all: a run that opened
//! no answer has no word to act on. `AbateUnreleased` is the consent -- the one
//! refusal in this arc that guards a person's word against a plan eager to read
//! more into it than they wrote. `AbateMismatched` is the whole point.
//! `AbateMiscounted` is the honesty of the count: every opened answer is abated
//! or held, never both and never neither. `AbateMisrecorded` is the record and
//! the wall together, both read back out of the plan's own notes rather than out
//! of the memory of having moved them.
//!
//! **An abatement survives a provisioning, and the wall it took down stays
//! down** -- proven as its own fold, since a boundary that returned every
//! morning would make the release a courtesy rather than an act.
//!
//! Usage:
//!   abate            Run the self-test (default)
//!   abate selftest   Same as default
//!   abate dependent ...  Run as one supervised dependent (spawned by the run)

